mode: "100644"
text: "# Operating Model — making the strategy run\n\nA strategy you can't sustain is a wish. This is how the choices become a working, repeatable\nengine — and how the brand actually wins, not just shows up.\n\n## The strategic wedge — how this brand wins\n\nShowing up with \"good content\" is table stakes; it's not a strategy. The **wedge** is the\ndifferentiated reason this brand wins attention on its chosen channels, and it comes straight\nfrom the brand's POV and positioning (`brand-profile`). A wedge is usually one of:\n\n- **A distinct point of view** — a stance the brand consistently takes that others won't\n  (\"radical transparency,\" \"build less\").\n- **An underserved angle** — \"the only one teaching X *for* Y\" (a specific audience others\n  ignore).\n- **A signature format** — a recognizable, repeatable content form the brand owns.\n- **A voice/personality** — a way of sounding that stands out in a beige feed.\n\nName the wedge explicitly. If you can't articulate why this brand wins beyond \"we'll post good\nstuff,\" the positioning needs sharpening first — surface that.\n\n## The content engine\n\nStrategy wires the other skills into a repeatable flow so the brand isn't starting from zero each\nweek:\n\n```\nFoundation (brand-profile · voice · audience)\n      ↓\nPillars (content-pillars)            ← the themes\n      ↓\nPlan (batch-content-plan)            ← the calendar\n      ↓\nProduce (caption/reels/hook/…)       ← the posts\n      ↓\nRepurpose (cross-platform-repurposing) ← multiply\n      ↓\nSchedule (scheduling-and-queue)      ← ship\n      ↓\nEngage (community skills)            ← the relationship\n```\n\nThe strategy's job is to make sure this engine points at the chosen objective and channels.\n\n## Cadence — realistic, sustainable\n\nSet a cadence per chosen channel that the brand can actually keep. **Consistency beats volume**:\na steady, modest cadence sustained for a year builds more than a burst that collapses in a month.\nDerive cadence from capacity (see below), not ambition. (Detailed cadence guidance lives in\n`batch-content-plan/references/calendar-and-cadence.md`.)\n\n## Phasing — start focused, then expand\n\nDon't launch everywhere on day one. Phase it:\n\n- **Phase 1 — Win one channel.** Pick the single best-fit platform, commit a sustainable cadence,\n  and get genuinely good at it. Establish the pillars, voice, and engine here.\n- **Phase 2 — Extend efficiently.** Add a secondary channel, fed largely by\n  `cross-platform-repurposing` so it doesn't double the workload.\n- **Phase 3 — Expand deliberately.** Add channels only as capacity and results justify.\n\nMost brands overreach in Phase 1 and stall. Narrow focus early is the unlock.\n\n## Capacity & roles\n\nBe explicit about what the brand can produce and who does it:\n\n- Who creates content, who engages/replies, how much time per week.\n- How much is net-new vs repurposed (repurposing is what makes a small team's cadence viable).\n- The honest constraint: plan to the time that actually exists, then use the engine (pillars +\n  batching + repurposing) to get the most from it.\n\nA focused strategy on one channel, run by one person sustainably, beats an ambitious multi-channel\nplan that burns out. Build the engine to the capacity, not the fantasy.\n"
